<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holdfast Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Holdfast Bay is a bay on the eastern shore of Gulf St Vincent in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, now lined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> suburbs of Glenelg and Brighton. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hindmarsh">John Hindmarsh</a> proclaimed the Province of South Australia there on 28 December 1836, a date still marked each year as Proclamation Day. The Old Gum Tree at Glenelg North marks the traditional site of the ceremony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holdfast-bay.jpg" alt="Holdfast Bay" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was AtD (Adam Trevorrow) at en.wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GlenelgBeach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-light">Colonel William Light</a>, surveyor general of South Australia, named the bay in mid-1836; his journal records his pleasure at the quality of the anchorage after riding out a storm there. The pioneers who were to found the colony landed at Holdfast Bay in 1836 and 1837. On 8 November 1836 Robert Gouger, the Colonial Secretary and Chief Magistrate, arrived aboard the Africaine and set up camp near the Old Gum Tree.</p><p>With Governor Hindmarsh's arrival and proclamation of the colony on 28 December, the settlement at Holdfast Bay became the first seat of government of South Australia, and it remained so until mid-March 1837. On 31 December 1836 the settlement was renamed Glenelg. The Corporate Town of Glenelg, later the City of Glenelg, was established in 1855 to govern the township and its surroundings, and the local government area along the shore today is the City of Holdfast Bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The small bay lies in Gulf St Vincent next to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> and holds both natural and artificial reefs. The Brighton and Glenelg jetties and The Blocks, a disused maritime structure of concrete and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> columns that now acts as a broken breakwater and reef, can be reached by swimming from the shore. A reef of tyre tetrahedrons was laid about 5 km west of Glenelg in 1983, and two vessels, the Glenelg barge and the South Australian, were scuttled in 15 to 20 metres of water.</p><p>The natural reefs lie between 8 and 24 metres deep. Among them are Devil's Elbow, where two shelves meet at a right angle and giant Australian cuttlefish are found; Fred's Rock, at 24 metres, part of a ground that includes the wreck of the Claris; and Seacliff Reef, the remains of a shoreline about 10,000 years old, which supports the largest known population of western blue devils in the waters around metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Holdfast Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first settlers arrived in 1836 and Governor John Hindmarsh proclaimed the province at <b>Holdfast Bay</b> on 28 December.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-south-australia">Province of South Australia</a></li></ul></section>
